00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:14.919
When you think of freight transport, the image
in your head might include massive container

00:00:14.919 --> 00:00:21.540
ships, or mile-long trains, or enormous cargo
planes, but more than each of those, the transportation

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:26.940
method that carries the vast majority of the
world’s freight is trucks.

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:33.500
In most places, it’s not even close—by
value, they carry 73% of the US’ domestic

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:34.500
freight.

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:39.080
It is truly trucks that connect the world,
more than anything else.

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:43.390
Trucking fills a lot of roles from just daily
deliveries to urban households all the way

00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:47.730
to cross-country treks bringing goods from
manufacturer to consumer.

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:53.080
It is that form, though—long-haul, over-the-road
trucking—that is most elusive.

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:57.510
These trucks are visible on the highways of
any country, but the infrastructure supporting

00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:01.600
this transportation system hides in plain
sight.

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:06.299
When on the road, long-haul truckers live
and work in their trucks.

00:01:06.299 --> 00:01:10.960
Of course their job is centered around sitting
behind the wheel, but the section behind that

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:12.780
is where they live.

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:17.600
Drivers are only allowed to drive a certain
number of hours at a time—in the US, it’s

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:21.799
11, in Europe, it’s 9, and in the rest of
the world it’s something similar to those

00:01:21.799 --> 00:01:22.799
two.

00:01:22.799 --> 00:01:27.560
Therefore, they need the facilities to live,
rest, and recreate when off the road in the

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:29.860
remaining hours of the day.

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:34.720
Most trucks have a cab just large enough for
a bed, TV, microwave, and a few other small

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:35.720
appliances.

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:40.590
They are typically intentionally minimalistic
because there is a trade-off between the size

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:44.070
and weight of the cab and how much freight
can be hauled.

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:48.439
This truly is a trade-off for the drivers,
mostly, as a big proportion of them in places

00:01:48.439 --> 00:01:54.180
like the US actually own their truck themselves
and work for companies on a freelance basis.

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:58.510
That means they could decide to buy a rig
with a larger, more comfortable cab, but that

00:01:58.510 --> 00:02:03.350
could make them less capable of hauling heavier
loads, which means less money.

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:07.330
Trucks are weight-restricted mostly to keep
the roads in decent condition.

00:02:07.330 --> 00:02:10.070
The greater the weight, the greater the impact.

00:02:10.070 --> 00:02:13.150
In the US, this maximum weight varies widely.

00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:19.060
On most highways it’s 80,000 pounds or 36,000
kilograms, but this varies from state to state

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:24.970
all the way up to 164,000 pounds or 75,000
kilograms in Michigan.

00:02:24.970 --> 00:02:29.150
Considering this variance, states will typically
have weigh stations near their borders on

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:32.980
major highways, as well as some spread out
throughout the state.

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:37.550
All trucks, except for some that have electric
systems allowing them to bypass, are required

00:02:37.550 --> 00:02:42.200
to pull off onto massive scales to assure
they’re complying with the rules.

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:46.090
Using this opportunity, though, many weigh
stations have become far more technologically

00:02:46.090 --> 00:02:49.400
advanced to assure safety and compliance.

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:53.129
For example, thermal cameras will take an
image of the wheels to assure the breaks are

00:02:53.129 --> 00:02:56.940
working properly—if they’re too cold,
that could mean the breaks are not engaging,

00:02:56.940 --> 00:03:00.599
if they’re too hot, that could mean they’re
engaging too much, in comparison to the other

00:03:00.599 --> 00:03:01.599
breaks.

00:03:01.599 --> 00:03:08.310
So, the weight issue, though, is why the average
rig looks just like this—a compact cab with

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:10.920
just the bare minimum of comforts.

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:15.680
The trade-off means that larger, extended
sleepers are only common with, for example,

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:20.180
long-haul moving trucks, which, due to the
low weight and density of the carried goods,

00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:22.410
rarely have an issue with weight limits.

00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:27.940
So, given that most trucks look like this,
with just the bare bones of comforts, most

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:33.530
of the facilities truckers need are located
in the places where they sleep—truck stops.

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:38.190
These dot large countries like the US with
remarkable frequency, centered primarily along

00:03:38.190 --> 00:03:40.740
highways and other common trucking routes.

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:45.739
They might look like large gas stations, but
they offer far more.

00:03:45.739 --> 00:03:50.659
Truck cabs typically don’t have bathrooms
or showers, so they almost always have those.

00:03:50.659 --> 00:03:54.940
They also don’t have laundry facilities,
so truck stops often have those.

00:03:54.940 --> 00:04:00.530
Some will also have banks, barbers, chiropractors,
dentists, dog groomers, and more.

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:05.180
This is all because, once truckers are on
the road, they’re in the system, and they

00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:08.190
can’t really leave it until they’re back
at their origin.

00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:12.489
One can’t just drive into a city and park
to do some shopping in a semi-truck, both

00:04:12.489 --> 00:04:15.190
practically and, in some cases, legally.

00:04:15.190 --> 00:04:19.819
While truckers might travel more miles in
a year than anyone else, the world, for them,

00:04:19.819 --> 00:04:24.809
while on the job, is limited to the roads,
truck-stops, and few other facilities catering

00:04:24.809 --> 00:04:25.809
to them.

00:04:25.809 --> 00:04:30.309
That’s why services like dentists at truck
stops are far from novelties.

00:04:30.309 --> 00:04:34.009
They’re crucial fixtures of a life on the
road.

00:04:34.009 --> 00:04:37.279
Truck drivers only make money when they’re
moving.

00:04:37.279 --> 00:04:42.389
Overwhelmingly, they are paid per mile meaning
the time during which they are legally mandated

00:04:42.389 --> 00:04:45.030
to rest is unpaid.

00:04:45.030 --> 00:04:48.600
Not only that, but time off actually loses
money.

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:51.419
The whole game is an economic puzzle.

00:04:51.419 --> 00:04:56.580
Trucks need power to run AC, heat, or electronics
while stationary—all of which drivers typically

00:04:56.580 --> 00:04:58.550
need when they’re stopped.

00:04:58.550 --> 00:05:03.860
Therefore, often, drivers leave their trucks
idling overnight which burns about 0.8 gallons

00:05:03.860 --> 00:05:06.449
or 3 liters of diesel per hour.

00:05:06.449 --> 00:05:12.229
At current prices, a 10-hour overnight stop
would burn about $20 of diesel which, multiplied

00:05:12.229 --> 00:05:16.879
by hundreds of nights per year per driver,
is a significant cost and an even greater

00:05:16.879 --> 00:05:18.360
environmental burden.

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:23.569
In addition, this idling adds wear and tear
to the engine, and counts against a truck’s

00:05:23.569 --> 00:05:24.569
warranty.

00:05:24.569 --> 00:05:28.779
Until recently, there really wasn’t an alternative,
except for sleeping without AC or heat, but

00:05:28.779 --> 00:05:33.899
under increased pressure to improve its environmental
footprint, the trucking industry has implemented

00:05:33.899 --> 00:05:38.740
innovations such as auxiliary power units—smaller,
more efficient generators—electric plug-ins

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:43.219
at truck stops, or even ducts installed to
the windows when stopped to provide climate

00:05:43.219 --> 00:05:44.219
control.

00:05:44.219 --> 00:05:47.300
This economic calculation extends beyond stops.

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:51.249
It also deals with optimizing the miles covered
while following the laws.

00:05:51.249 --> 00:05:56.409
In the US, the rule is that one can drive
up to 11 hours, over an overall period of

00:05:56.409 --> 00:06:00.229
14 hours, and then must rest for 10 hours.

00:06:00.229 --> 00:06:04.789
Assuming an average speed of 60 miles per
hour, that means a truck can essentially travel

00:06:04.789 --> 00:06:07.369
660 miles per day.

00:06:07.369 --> 00:06:12.360
Therefore, a cross-country trip from Los Angeles
to New York would take about four and a half

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:13.550
days.

00:06:13.550 --> 00:06:18.089
The drivers themselves are paid per mile so
all they want to do is maximize their speed

00:06:18.089 --> 00:06:21.009
during the 11 hours of daily driving time.

00:06:21.009 --> 00:06:25.300
The consumer, though—whoever’s paying
for the transport of the product in the back—cares

00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:28.259
about how fast it gets to them overall.

00:06:28.259 --> 00:06:33.339
Therefore, truckers have devised a system
to move freight faster.

00:06:33.339 --> 00:06:38.089
The rules just say that drivers have to rest
for ten hours in their cab—it doesn’t

00:06:38.089 --> 00:06:40.699
say that the trucks have to be stationary.

00:06:40.699 --> 00:06:45.349
Drivers will therefore team up so that while
one drives, the other sleeps, and vice versa.

00:06:45.349 --> 00:06:50.620
A team of drivers, which is often made up
of a married couple, can therefore drive 22

00:06:50.620 --> 00:06:54.009
hours per day, covering 1,320 miles.

00:06:54.009 --> 00:06:59.340
That means that they could make the Los Angeles
to New York trip in just two days.

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:03.270
Consumers will pay a premium for the speed,
and therefore trucking companies will pay

00:07:03.270 --> 00:07:04.490
more per mile.

00:07:04.490 --> 00:07:09.370
In addition, having less stopped time means
that the truck, which costs quite a lot, is

00:07:09.370 --> 00:07:11.610
being used more efficiently.

00:07:11.610 --> 00:07:17.020
This team system is all-around a win for everyone,
assuming one can find a pair of people that

00:07:17.020 --> 00:07:20.930
will tolerate spending their lives cooped
up in a truck cab together.

00:07:20.930 --> 00:07:25.539
Truckers, almost certainly, are a dying breed,
though.

00:07:25.539 --> 00:07:31.089
The future of freight transport will undoubtably
center around self-driving trucks and the

00:07:31.089 --> 00:07:34.279
technology for these is very close to ready
for prime-time.

00:07:34.279 --> 00:07:40.349
Right now, multiple companies are at the point
where their trucks are traveling autonomously,

00:07:40.349 --> 00:07:43.770
with drivers behind the wheel to intervene
if needed.

00:07:43.770 --> 00:07:48.879
These are essentially autopilot systems, similar
to Tesla’s, that aid the driver and reduce

00:07:48.879 --> 00:07:49.879
fatigue.

00:07:49.879 --> 00:07:55.020
Soon, though, with more proven technology
and legal authority to do so, this will likely

00:07:55.020 --> 00:07:57.710
advance to autonomous convoys.

00:07:57.710 --> 00:08:02.339
In this system, one driver would sit behind
the wheel of a lead truck, which might also

00:08:02.339 --> 00:08:07.959
drive autonomously, and then a series of autonomous,
human-less trucks would follow behind.

00:08:07.959 --> 00:08:12.439
This way, there is still a human that can
assist with mechanical issues or unforeseen

00:08:12.439 --> 00:08:17.580
road conditions, but the freight to driver
ratio increases dramatically.

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:22.909
This will, of course, reduce the number of
truckers needed, but then, after this phase,

00:08:22.909 --> 00:08:27.069
the industry will undoubtably advance to fully
autonomous trucks.

00:08:27.069 --> 00:08:32.229
At first, this will likely just be on dedicated
routes—for example, a company might deploy

00:08:32.229 --> 00:08:38.020
these on uncomplicated routes from one warehouse
to another, both being close to a highway.

00:08:38.020 --> 00:08:42.599
Autonomous driving on highways is relatively
easy since roads conditions are standardized

00:08:42.599 --> 00:08:44.169
and predictable.

00:08:44.169 --> 00:08:48.950
Autonomous city driving is much more complicated,
so human drivers might still be needed for

00:08:48.950 --> 00:08:51.459
the start and end of journeys.

00:08:51.459 --> 00:08:56.170
What fully autonomous driving could do, though,
is allow a truck to continue moving on the

00:08:56.170 --> 00:09:00.750
highway while a driver rests in the back,
allowing essentially continuous driving without

00:09:00.750 --> 00:09:03.180
having to deal with working hour limits.

00:09:03.180 --> 00:09:08.860
This, along with a convoy system, could increase
the cost efficiency of trucking dramatically,

00:09:08.860 --> 00:09:11.779
as the human drivers represent a significant
cost.

00:09:11.779 --> 00:09:17.190
An average trucker’s pay is about 35 cents
per mile, meaning that cutting out that cost

00:09:17.190 --> 00:09:23.160
entirely would reduce the cost of a Los Angeles
to New York trip, for example, by almost $1,000.

00:09:23.160 --> 00:09:28.490
It is for this reason why so many companies
are pursuing autonomous trucks.

00:09:28.490 --> 00:09:34.600
Of course, the other major cost in trucking
is fuel, so companies are also trying to innovate

00:09:34.600 --> 00:09:36.089
in that space.

00:09:36.089 --> 00:09:41.120
Concurrent to the deployment of autonomous
trucks is a likely shift into the use of electric

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:42.120
trucks.

00:09:42.120 --> 00:09:46.200
Tesla, for example, is developing its own
semi truck.

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:51.050
They claim its energy consumption will be
about two kilowatt hours per mile.

00:09:51.050 --> 00:09:55.620
The US’ average energy cost is about 10
cents per kilowatt hour, which translates

00:09:55.620 --> 00:09:59.120
to an energy cost of about 20 cents per mile.

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:03.620
Typical internal combustion semi-trucks have
an efficiency of about 6 miles per gallon

00:10:03.620 --> 00:10:09.650
so, at current diesel prices, that would translate
to a cost of about 40 cents per mile.

00:10:09.650 --> 00:10:14.920
Halving a trucking company’s energy prices
is certainly an intriguing proposition, which

00:10:14.920 --> 00:10:18.920
is why many consider the Tesla semi so promising.

00:10:18.920 --> 00:10:24.329
At those prices, if a truck goes driverless,
meaning no salary costs, the variable cost

00:10:24.329 --> 00:10:30.649
of a cross-country, Los Angeles to New York
run would be just $550.

00:10:30.649 --> 00:10:35.769
To be able to haul tens of thousands of pounds
of goods from door to door across the country

00:10:35.769 --> 00:10:41.320
for just hundreds of dollars would result
in a paradigm shift in the freight world.

00:10:41.320 --> 00:10:45.220
There are certainly disadvantages, such as
the high battery pack weight that reduces

00:10:45.220 --> 00:10:50.240
cargo capacity, but it is clear that electric
trucks will play some role in the industry

00:10:50.240 --> 00:10:52.579
regardless in the near future.

00:10:52.579 --> 00:10:56.960
This role will likely be concentrated in shorter
routes, rather than long-haul ones, but this

00:10:56.960 --> 00:11:01.810
is still a significant enough subset of the
industry that electric trucks have the potential

00:11:01.810 --> 00:11:03.350
for significant disruption.

00:11:03.350 --> 00:11:10.000
Right now, there is an strange duality where
truckers are so crucial the running of a modern

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:13.450
economy that the world is working to replace
them.

00:11:13.450 --> 00:11:17.839
We can’t live without them right now, so
we’re finding a way to.

00:11:17.839 --> 00:11:23.930
In the future, though, the job of a trucker
will undoubtably be another victim of an increasingly

00:11:23.930 --> 00:11:27.310
modernized world.

